COPY public.node__field_definition (bundle, deleted, entity_id, revision_id, langcode, delta, field_definition_value, field_definition_format) FROM stdin;
term	0	1	1	en	0	<p>A special text file that tells Git which files or folders should not be tracked or saved (for example passwords or temporary system files).</p>	basic_html
term	0	2	2	en	0	<p>A command shell for Unix-based operating systems. It lets you run commands, write scripts and manage the system through a text interface.</p>	basic_html
term	0	3	3	en	0	<p>Redirects error messages (stderr, stream 2) to /dev/null, which discards them, so errors are not shown in the terminal.</p>	basic_html
term	0	4	4	en	0	<p>A family of operating systems that Linux and macOS are based on.</p>	basic_html
term	0	5	5	en	0	<p>A free operating system built on the Linux kernel. Its source code is open, so anyone can view, modify and distribute it. It is stable, secure and highly customizable, and is widely used on personal computers, web servers and embedded systems.</p>	basic_html
term	0	6	6	en	0	<p>A separate line of development that lets you build new features or fix bugs without affecting the main code.</p>	basic_html
term	0	7	7	en	0	<p>Downloading a full copy of a code repository from the cloud (for example from GitHub) to your local computer so you can work on it.</p>	basic_html
term	0	8	8	en	0	<p>Saving the state of the code at a specific point in time. It creates a record in the project history with a message describing the changes.</p>	basic_html
term	0	9	9	en	0	<p>A platform for packaging applications together with their environment into containers, so they run the same way on any machine.</p>	basic_html
term	0	10	10	en	0	<p>Creating a personal copy of a repository in your own GitHub account. It lets you work on someone else's code without affecting the original project.</p>	basic_html
term	0	11	11	en	0	<p>A version control system. It tracks changes in code and lets multiple developers work on the same project without overwriting each other's changes.</p>	basic_html
term	0	12	12	en	0	<p>A cloud service for hosting and managing code based on Git. It provides tools for collaboration, code review and issue tracking.</p>	basic_html
term	0	13	13	en	0	<p>Combining changes from one branch into another. Usually used to bring finished work back into the main branch of the project.</p>	basic_html
term	0	14	14	en	0	<p>The | operator. It passes the output of one command as the input of the next command.</p>	basic_html
term	0	15	15	en	0	<p>A formal request to merge your changes into the original project or the main branch. It lets other developers review the code (Code Review) before it is approved.</p>	basic_html
term	0	16	16	en	0	<p>Push uploads your commits from your computer to the remote server. Pull downloads updates made by others from the server to your computer.</p>	basic_html
term	0	17	17	en	0	<p>A central storage location that holds all the files, code and change history of a project.</p>	basic_html
term	0	18	18	en	0	<p>A Linux command that shuffles or randomly selects lines. Example: shuf -e {A..Z} -n 1 picks one random uppercase letter from A to Z.</p>	basic_html
term	0	19	19	en	0	<p>A program that provides a text interface (CLI) for communicating with the operating system. It is used to run commands, scripts and programs.</p>	basic_html
